window.SAME_FOREST_SPREADS = [{"date":"2026-09-22","doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 800 320'><defs><linearGradient id='g22' x1='0' x2='1'><stop offset='0' stop-color='var(--blue)'/><stop offset='1' stop-color='var(--marigold)'/><\/linearGradient><\/defs><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='320' fill='var(--blue)'/><rect x='400' y='0' width='400' height='320' fill='var(--marigold)'/><rect x='340' y='0' width='120' height='320' fill='url(#g22)'/><g fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><circle cx='170' cy='170' r='15.0'/><path d='M170 185.0 L 170 219.0'/><path d='M170 195.0 L 154.0 213.0 M170 195.0 L 186.0 213.0'/><path d='M170 219.0 L 140.0 229.0 L 166.0 237.0 M170 219.0 L 200.0 229.0 L 174.0 237.0'/><path d='M110 90 q 20 -40 60 -20 q 30 -30 60 0 q 40 -10 30 30 q 20 30 -20 30 q -20 30 -60 10 q -40 20 -60 -10 q -30 -10 -10 -40 z' stroke-width='2.5' opacity='.85'/><path d='M126 78 q 30 20 60 -6 q 30 24 56 0 q 20 22 -8 34 q -34 14 -60 -4 q -30 18 -48 -10' stroke-width='2' opacity='.55'/><path d='M262 100 q 40 10 80 40 M258 118 q 50 20 90 60 M250 134 q 40 30 70 80' stroke-width='2' opacity='.55'/><path d='M342 140 L 590 168 M348 178 L 596 172 M320 214 L 600 178' stroke-width='2' opacity='.55'/><rect x='620' y='190' width='90' height='50' rx='4'/><circle cx='640' cy='258' r='16'/><circle cx='695' cy='258' r='16'/><path d='M620 212 L 556 214'/><ellipse cx='520' cy='218' rx='42' ry='18'/><path d='M556 208 L 578 176'/><ellipse cx='586' cy='172' rx='15' ry='8'/><path d='M498 234 L 492 266 M512 236 L 510 266 M532 236 L 536 266 M548 232 L 556 266 M480 214 q -18 6 -20 22' stroke-width='2.5'/><circle cx='665' cy='110' r='15.0'/><path d='M665 125.0 L 665 159.0'/><path d='M665 135.0 L 651.0 119.0 M665 135.0 L 679.0 119.0'/><path d='M665 159.0 L 653.0 199.0 M665 159.0 L 677.0 199.0'/><path d='M690 120 q 30 30 0 60' stroke-width='2.5'/><path d='M690 120 L 690 180' stroke-width='1.5' opacity='.7'/><\/g><g transform='translate(580,185.0) scale(1.0)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"left":{"lineage":"buddhist","source":"mingyur rinpoche, the joy of living · read chapter 1, he tells it himself","sourceRef":"rinpoche","text":"sit down.\n[[rinpoche|mingyur rinpoche]] had [[panic|panic attacks]] as a kid. real ones, the kind where you think you're dying. at thirteen he went into a [[retreat|three year retreat]] and for the first year they got worse.\nthen he quit fighting them. he let the panic in and watched it. it fell apart in a few days. it had needed the fight to stay alive."},"mode":"disagree","question":"is this the kind of sitting that dissolves the thing, or the kind that keeps it company?","refs":{"arjuna":{"lines":"the best archer alive, third of five brothers, and the one the whole gita is talking to.\nthe war is a family fight over a kingdom. his cousins are on the other side, along with the great-uncle who raised him and the teacher who taught him to shoot.\nhe asks krishna to drive out between the armies so he can look. he looks, his hands go weak, the bow slides out, and he sits down in the chariot and says he won't fight.\nnobody in the story treats that as cowardice except krishna, for about three verses, and then even he moves on to something better. the sitting down is real. so is the getting up. the book needs both.","name":"arjuna","where":"bhagavad gita, chapter 1 for sitting down, chapter 2 for the rest."},"gita":{"lines":"seven hundred verses tucked into the mahabharata, the enormous indian war epic. eighteen chapters, all one conversation, held on a battlefield in the minute before the fighting starts.\nhindus read it as scripture. gandhi read it most mornings. ram dass's whole path runs through it.\nchapter 2 is where krishna starts talking, and the first thing he says is not gentle. read the first ten verses before the famous ones and you'll see the tone the rest of the book is correcting for.","name":"the bhagavad gita","where":"bhagavad gita, chapter 2, verses 1 through 10, then keep going. any translation you can hold."},"krishna":{"lines":"arjuna's charioteer, friend, and cousin on his mother's side. he agreed to drive but not to fight.\nsomewhere in chapter two it becomes clear he is also god, and in chapter eleven arjuna asks to see that directly and immediately wishes he hadn't.\nbut before any of that, right at the top of chapter two, he's just a friend watching another friend fall apart at the worst possible moment, and he says the thing friends say. get up. this isn't you.\nthe kindness comes after. the first thing out of his mouth is a shove.","name":"krishna","where":"bhagavad gita 2:1-10 for the shove. the rest of chapter 2 for what comes after it."},"panic":{"lines":"he describes them starting around age seven or eight. his heart would race, he couldn't breathe, he was sure he was about to die. they came most in crowds and when he had to sit in the front at ceremonies, which as a recognized young teacher was constantly.\nhis father taught him to meditate at nine, partly for this. it helped and it didn't. the panic was still there, waiting.\nwhat finally worked is the part he tells over and over, because it's the whole teaching: he stopped treating the panic as an enemy and started treating it as a guest. he looked straight at it. and it turned out to be made of nothing in particular, once it wasn't being pushed against.","name":"the panic attacks","where":"the joy of living, chapter 1, and the introduction. he tells it plainly."},"retreat":{"lines":"in tibetan buddhism there's a traditional intensive: three years, three months, three days, in a closed retreat with a small group, doing nothing but practice. it's how the tradition trains its teachers.\nhe went in at thirteen, the youngest in his group, at sherab ling monastery in india. he says the first year was the worst of his life. the panic had nowhere to go and neither did he.\nsomewhere in that year he made the choice to stop fighting, and by his account the attacks were gone within days and never came back.\nhe was so young when he came out that they sent him back in as the retreat master for the next group. he was seventeen.","name":"the three year retreat","where":"the joy of living, chapter 1. he keeps the retreat details short; the turn is the point."},"rinpoche":{"lines":"a tibetan buddhist teacher, born in nepal in 1975, the son of one of the great meditation masters of the last century, tulku urgyen rinpoche. rinpoche is a title, it means precious one. he was recognized as a reincarnated teacher as a small boy.\nnone of that stopped the panic attacks. he's open about it, which is rare for someone in his position, and it's why people trust him.\nhe's been wired up in neuroscience labs more than almost any meditator alive, which is where the newspapers got the line about the happiest man in the world. he laughs at that.\nin 2011 he walked out of his monastery with no money and no plan and wandered india as a beggar for four and a half years. he nearly died of food poisoning in the first few weeks. he came back and wrote about it. he runs a network of meditation centers called tergar.","name":"yongey mingyur rinpoche","where":"the joy of living, 2007, for the panic and the retreat. in love with the world, 2019, for the wandering years."}},"right":{"lineage":"hindu","source":"bhagavad gita 2:2-3 · read the first ten verses of chapter 2, before he gets kind","sourceRef":"gita","text":"get up.\nthat's what [[krishna]] says to [[arjuna]] the second he opens his mouth. before the philosophy, before any of it. don't be a coward, it doesn't suit you, get up.\nsitting with it is not the same as sitting it out. sometimes the thing you're watching so carefully is just you, not moving."},"theme":"sit down, get up","id":"2026-09-22","caption":"the cloud he quit fighting comes apart, and the loose ends turn out to be reins."},{"date":"2026-09-23","doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 800 320'><defs><linearGradient id='g23' x1='0' x2='1'><stop offset='0' stop-color='var(--peach)'/><stop offset='1' stop-color='var(--marigold)'/><\/linearGradient><\/defs><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='320' fill='var(--peach)'/><rect x='400' y='0' width='400' height='320' fill='var(--marigold)'/><rect x='340' y='0' width='120' height='320' fill='url(#g23)'/><g fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><circle cx='140' cy='120' r='15.0'/><path d='M140 135.0 L 140 169.0'/><path d='M140 145.0 L 124.0 163.0 M140 145.0 L 156.0 163.0'/><path d='M140 169.0 L 128.0 209.0 M140 169.0 L 152.0 209.0'/><path d='M110 175 q 30 -20 60 0 l -6 30 q -24 8 -48 0 z'/><path d='M200 268 l 14 -10 l 10 12 z M240 274 l 12 -8 l 8 10 z M290 270 l 14 -9 l 9 11 z M340 272 l 12 -8 l 9 10 z' stroke-width='2.5'/><path d='M0 288 L 400 288' opacity='.4'/><path d='M405 274 q 14 -10 28 0 M445 276 q 14 -10 28 0 M485 274 q 14 -10 28 0 M525 276 q 14 -10 28 0' stroke-width='2.5'/><circle cx='660' cy='120' r='15'/><path d='M660 135 L 660 190'/><path d='M660 150 L 646 168 M660 150 L 674 168'/><path d='M660 190 L 704 190 L 704 226 M700 226 L 714 226'/><path d='M640 150 L 640 192 L 700 192'/><circle cx='652' cy='232' r='30'/><circle cx='712' cy='250' r='10'/><path d='M400 288 L 800 288' opacity='.4'/><\/g><g transform='translate(668,172.0) scale(1.2)' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><path d='M0 5 Q 4 2 8 3 L 26 3 L 26 8 L 20 8 L 20 11 L 24 11 L 24 15 L 6 15 L 6 11 L 10 11 L 10 8 L 4 8 Z'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"left":{"lineage":"christian","source":"richard rohr, falling upward · read the introduction, it's the whole argument","sourceRef":"falling","text":"you don't grow from doing it right.\n[[rohr]]'s whole book [[falling|falling upward]] is one point. the first half of your life builds the container. the second half only starts when something breaks it.\nnobody signs up for the breaking. you get thrown."},"mode":"agree","question":"what broke that you're still trying to glue back?","refs":{"falling":{"lines":"rohr's argument is that there are two halves to a life, and they are not the same job.\nthe first half is building the container. identity, career, family, beliefs, a sense of being somebody. you're supposed to do this, and you're supposed to take it seriously.\nthe second half is what the container was for. and here's the catch he's built the book around: almost nobody moves into the second half on purpose. you get moved. failure, loss, illness, a marriage ending, something you can't fix. he calls it necessary suffering.\nthe title is the point. the way up is the way down. you fall into the second half of your life. you don't climb.","name":"falling upward","where":"falling upward: a spirituality for the two halves of life. chapter 5, on necessary suffering, if you only read one."},"ramdass":{"lines":"born richard alpert, a harvard psychology professor who got fired in 1963 alongside timothy leary for giving psychedelics to students.\nhe went to india in 1967 looking for somebody who knew what the drugs had shown him, and found an old man in a blanket named neem karoli baba, who read his mind and told him things nobody could have known. he never really came back. the old man named him ram dass, servant of god.\nhe wrote be here now in 1971, a book that looks like a hippie art project and is still in print because it works. he spent the next fifty years talking about love, service, and dying, and he was funny the whole time.\nhe died in maui in december 2019, on purpose, at home, surrounded, with a lot of people watching him do it well.","name":"ram dass","where":"be here now for the beginning. still here for after the stroke. his talks are collected by the love serve remember foundation."},"rohr":{"lines":"a franciscan friar, which means a catholic priest in the order started by francis of assisi, the one who talked to birds and gave everything away.\nrohr has spent fifty years in new mexico running a place called the center for action and contemplation, teaching a version of christianity that's more interested in what happens inside you than in who's in and who's out.\nhe writes a lot. falling upward, the universal christ, everything belongs. he's the one who gave a lot of people permission to stay in the tradition without swallowing all of it.\nhe doesn't get everybody all the way there. but he gets you out of the parking lot.","name":"richard rohr","where":"falling upward, 2011. the introduction and the first two chapters carry the whole idea."},"stroke":{"lines":"february 1997. he was sixty five, lying in bed thinking about how he'd write about aging, and a blood vessel in his brain let go. he was found on the floor. paralyzed on the right side, most of his speech gone.\nhe said later that in the moment he looked for the thing he'd taught for thirty years, the witness, the calm center, and it wasn't there. he was just a scared old man on a floor. that was the first lesson.\nhe spent years learning to talk again. slowly. long pauses. his talks after the stroke are half silence, and people say those were his best ones, because he was no longer performing.\nfierce grace was the phrase he landed on. grace that doesn't ask, doesn't soften, and doesn't leave.","name":"the stroke","where":"still here, 2000, is the book he was writing when it happened, finished afterward. fierce grace, 2001, is the documentary. watch the film."}},"right":{"lineage":"hindu","source":"ram dass, still here · read the book, then find the film fierce grace and watch him try to talk","sourceRef":"stroke","text":"[[ramdass|ram dass]] had a [[stroke|stroke]] in 1997.\nit took his right side and most of his words. he called it fierce grace. not at first. at first he called it what you'd call it.\nbut the stroke did what forty years of meditating hadn't. it took away the guy who was good at talking about god, and left the one who had to sit there."},"theme":"what the break is for","id":"2026-09-23","caption":"the pieces of the pot are the stones of the path."},{"date":"2026-09-24","doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 800 320'><defs><linearGradient id='g24' x1='0' x2='1'><stop offset='0' stop-color='var(--sage)'/><stop offset='1' stop-color='var(--peach)'/><\/linearGradient><\/defs><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='320' fill='var(--sage)'/><rect x='400' y='0' width='400' height='320' fill='var(--peach)'/><rect x='340' y='0' width='120' height='320' fill='url(#g24)'/><g fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M0 270 q 60 -18 120 0 t 120 0 t 120 0 t 40 0' opacity='.55'/><path d='M20 292 q 60 -14 120 0 t 120 0 t 120 0 t 20 0' opacity='.3'/><circle cx='150' cy='120' r='15.0'/><path d='M150 135.0 L 150 169.0'/><path d='M150 145.0 L 136.0 129.0 M150 145.0 L 164.0 129.0'/><path d='M150 169.0 L 138.0 209.0 M150 169.0 L 162.0 209.0'/><rect x='100' y='70' width='100' height='70' rx='2'/><path d='M560 270 q -20 -40 10 -50 q 10 -30 40 -20 q 30 -20 40 10 q 30 0 10 30 q 10 30 -20 30 z'/><path d='M575 232 q 6 -20 -4 -30 q 14 4 12 22 M600 215 q 10 -22 -2 -34 q 18 6 14 26 M625 226 q 8 -18 -2 -28 q 14 6 10 22' stroke-width='2.5' opacity='.8'/><circle cx='690' cy='140' r='15.0'/><path d='M690 155.0 L 690 189.0'/><path d='M690 165.0 L 674.0 183.0 M690 165.0 L 706.0 183.0'/><path d='M690 189.0 L 678.0 229.0 M690 189.0 L 702.0 229.0'/><path d='M724 268 q 8 -4 16 0 M746 270 q 8 -4 16 0' stroke-width='2.5' opacity='.7'/><path d='M400 270 L 800 270' opacity='.55'/><\/g><g transform='translate(590,209.65) scale(1.05)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"left":{"lineage":"tao","source":"tao te ching, chapter 1 · read 1 through 4 in a row, any translation","sourceRef":"tao","text":"the second you name it, you missed it.\nfirst line of the [[tao|tao te ching]], about twenty five hundred years old: the way you can talk about isn't the way.\n[[laotzu|lao tzu]] opens his whole book by telling you the book can't do it. then he writes eighty more chapters anyway. that's the honest version of every scripture."},"mode":"agree","question":"what have you named that never told you its name?","refs":{"bush":{"lines":"moses is out with his father in law's sheep on a mountain called horeb, and a bush is on fire and not burning up. he goes over to look, which is the whole thing. he could have kept walking.\nthe voice tells him to take his sandals off, the ground is holy. then it tells him it has seen the misery of his people in egypt and it's sending him.\nwhen he asks for a name, the answer in hebrew is ehyeh asher ehyeh. i am who i am, or i will be what i will be. the verb is unfinished on purpose.\nfrom that answer the jewish tradition gets the four letter name it doesn't say out loud, and christians and muslims inherit the same refusal to pin the thing down. every argument about god's name starts at this bush.","name":"the burning bush","where":"exodus 3, the first fifteen verses, slowly."},"exodus":{"lines":"the second book of the bible. in hebrew it's called shemot, which means names, after its first line.\nit's the story of a people enslaved in egypt, the plagues, the escape through the sea, the mountain, the ten commandments, and a lot of instructions about building a tent.\nchapter 3 is where the whole thing turns. an old shepherd, a bush, a name that isn't one. everything after runs on that conversation.","name":"exodus","where":"exodus, chapter 3 today. chapters 1 through 20 for the story."},"laotzu":{"lines":"the name just means old master. nobody is sure he existed as one person.\nthe story goes that he kept the archives at the royal court, got tired of the whole thing, and rode west on a water buffalo to leave the country. the gatekeeper at the border recognized him and wouldn't let him through until he wrote down what he knew.\nhe wrote five thousand characters, handed them over, and rode off. nobody saw him again. that's the book.\nwhether it happened or not, it's the right story for a book that begins by saying words won't get you there.","name":"lao tzu","where":"sima qian's records of the grand historian, written around 100 bc, has the oldest version of the story. it's already a legend by then."},"moses":{"lines":"a hebrew baby raised in the egyptian palace after his mother floated him down the nile to save him from a killing order. he grew up a prince, killed an egyptian he saw beating a slave, and ran.\nhe spent forty years in the desert as a shepherd, married, raising kids, done with all of it. he was eighty when the bush caught fire.\nhe argued with the voice for two chapters. who am i. what's your name. they won't believe me. i can't talk right. send somebody else. every excuse got answered and he went.\nhe led the slaves out of egypt, spent forty more years in the desert with them, and died on a mountain looking at the land he never got to enter. the five books that open the bible are traditionally his.","name":"moses","where":"exodus, chapters 2 through 4 for the beginning. deuteronomy 34 for the end."},"tao":{"lines":"eighty one short chapters, about five thousand chinese characters in all. you can read the whole thing in an hour and spend the rest of your life on it.\ntao means the way. te means the power or virtue that comes from being lined up with it. ching means classic. so, the classic of the way and its power.\nit's the root text of taoism and it leaks into everything else in china, including zen, which is what you get when buddhism moves in next door to this book.\nit doesn't tell you what the way is. it says it can't. then it spends eighty chapters pointing: water, valleys, empty bowls, the space inside a wheel. the useful part is always the part that isn't there.","name":"the tao te ching","where":"tao te ching, chapters 1 through 4 to start. stephen mitchell's version reads easiest, ursula le guin's is the most honest about being a guess."}},"right":{"lineage":"christian","source":"exodus 3:14 · read all of chapter 3, and notice what moses tries to get out of","sourceRef":"exodus","text":"[[moses]] asked for a name.\nhe's barefoot in front of a [[bush|bush that's on fire and not burning up]], and he asks, who do i say sent me.\nthe answer is i am who i am. that's not a name. that's a refusal to be one.\nwe've spent three thousand years painting a face on it anyway."},"theme":"the name it never gave","id":"2026-09-24","caption":"the river won't stay in the frame. the bush won't burn up."},{"date":"2026-09-25","doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 800 320'><defs><linearGradient id='g25' x1='0' x2='1'><stop offset='0' stop-color='var(--peach)'/><stop offset='1' stop-color='var(--blue)'/><\/linearGradient><\/defs><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='320' fill='var(--peach)'/><rect x='400' y='0' width='400' height='320' fill='var(--blue)'/><rect x='340' y='0' width='120' height='320' fill='url(#g25)'/><g fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M20 250 L 380 250 M20 282 L 380 282' opacity='.45'/><path d='M30 300 L 380 300' opacity='.25'/><circle cx='230' cy='150' r='15.0'/><path d='M230 165.0 L 230 199.0'/><path d='M230 175.0 L 212.0 169.0 M230 175.0 L 248.0 185.0'/><path d='M230 199.0 L 204.0 229.0 L 196.0 241.0 M230 199.0 L 248.0 225.0 L 240.0 243.0'/><path d='M150 262 q 10 -6 20 0 M120 270 q 10 -6 20 0 M170 276 q 8 -5 16 0' stroke-width='2' opacity='.5'/><path d='M40 236 L 70 236 L 76 250 L 34 250 Z'/><circle cx='560' cy='150' r='15.0'/><path d='M560 165.0 L 560 199.0'/><path d='M560 175.0 L 544.0 193.0 M560 175.0 L 576.0 193.0'/><path d='M560 199.0 L 530.0 209.0 L 556.0 217.0 M560 199.0 L 590.0 209.0 L 564.0 217.0'/><ellipse cx='612' cy='214' rx='22' ry='16'/><path d='M632 206 L 700 176'/><path d='M612 214 L 400 250 M612 214 L 400 282 M612 214 L 400 300' stroke-width='2' opacity='.55'/><\/g><g transform='translate(42,209.0) scale(1.0)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"left":{"lineage":"christian","source":"1 corinthians 9:24-27 · read the whole chapter, he's answering a complaint","sourceRef":"corinthians","text":"run like you mean it.\n[[paul]] told the [[corinthians]] every runner runs but only one gets the prize, so run like that one.\nthen he says he beats his body and makes it his slave. that's the actual language. the man was not into balance."},"mode":"disagree","question":"which string are you tuning, and which one are you just yanking on?","refs":{"buddha":{"lines":"siddhartha gautama, a prince from a small kingdom on the edge of what's now nepal, about twenty five hundred years ago. raised behind palace walls, kept from seeing sickness, old age, or death. when he finally saw them, he left. wife, kid, everything, at twenty nine.\nfor six years he did the hardest practices he could find, starving himself down to skin over bone. it didn't work. he ate a meal, sat down under a tree, and woke up. that's what buddha means, the one who woke up.\nthe first thing he taught after that was the middle way. not the palace, not the starving. the lute string, before he ever said it to sona.\nhe taught for forty five years and died at eighty, lying between two trees, telling his monks to work out their own freedom.","name":"the buddha","where":"the pali canon, the oldest collection of his talks. for his own story, the ariyapariyesana sutta, majjhima nikaya 26."},"corinthians":{"lines":"corinth was a port city in greece, rich, rough, and famous for it. paul started a church there around the year 50 and stayed about a year and a half.\nafter he left, it fell apart in every direction. factions, lawsuits between members, a man sleeping with his stepmother, rich people eating the shared meal before the poor arrived, arguments about who had the better spiritual gifts.\nfirst corinthians is paul's answer to all of it. it's also where the famous love chapter lives, chapter 13, which people read at weddings without knowing it was written to people who couldn't stand each other.\nchapter 9 is him defending himself against members who questioned whether he was a real apostle. the runner line comes at the end of that argument.","name":"the corinthians","where":"1 corinthians, all sixteen chapters. read 9 for today, then 13 to see what he was actually after."},"paul":{"lines":"born saul, a pharisee from tarsus, a tentmaker by trade. he was on the road to damascus to arrest christians when a light knocked him flat and a voice asked why he was doing it. he was blind for three days.\nthen he spent the rest of his life starting small churches around the mediterranean and writing them letters when they got into trouble, which was constantly. thirteen letters in the new testament carry his name.\nhe was beaten, shipwrecked, stoned once and left for dead, and jailed more than once. tradition says rome finally executed him.\nhe used athletes as a picture of the spiritual life more than once. corinth hosted the isthmian games, second only to the olympics, so his readers had seen the runners.","name":"paul","where":"acts 9 for the road. 1 corinthians 9 for the runners. 2 corinthians 11 for what it cost him."},"sona":{"lines":"sona kolivisa, a rich young man from a merchant family who became a monk under the buddha. before that he'd been a musician, a lute player.\nhe threw himself at the practice the way he'd probably thrown himself at everything. he did walking meditation until the soles of his feet split open and the path was bloody, and he still wasn't free. he started thinking about quitting and going back to his money.\nthe buddha came to find him. instead of a lecture he asked about the lute. that conversation is the whole sutta.\nsona stayed, and the tradition says he got there. the lute simile is one of the most quoted things the buddha ever said about effort.","name":"sona","where":"the sona sutta, anguttara nikaya 6.55. it also shows up in the vinaya, the monks' rulebook, mahavagga 5."},"sutta":{"lines":"a sutta is one talk, or one conversation, that the buddha or one of his students gave. thousands of them were memorized and chanted for a few hundred years before anybody wrote them down.\nthe collection is the pali canon, named for the language it's in. it's sorted into groups. the anguttara nikaya is the numbered collection, where talks are grouped by how many things they list. the sona sutta lives in the book of sixes, which is why it's 6.55.\nnone of it is scripture in the sense of a rulebook handed down from the sky. it's a record of a man talking to people about how to suffer less. some of it is dry. some of it, like sona and his lute, is a story you can hold.","name":"what a sutta is","where":"anguttara nikaya, the book of sixes, sutta 55. any translation of the pali canon."}},"right":{"lineage":"buddhist","source":"sona sutta, anguttara nikaya 6.55 · read the whole conversation, it's short","sourceRef":"sutta","text":"loosen the string.\na monk named [[sona]] walked until his feet bled and still wasn't getting anywhere.\n[[buddha|the buddha]] didn't tell him to try harder. he asked about the lute sona used to play. too tight and the string snaps. too loose and there's no note.\ntune it. then play."},"theme":"how hard to push","id":"2026-09-25","caption":"the strings of the lute are the lanes he runs in."},{"date":"2026-09-26","doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 800 320' aria-label='a figure in a cell lit by a tiny window on the left, a small crowd at a graveside on the right, one light crossing both'><defs><linearGradient id='beam2' x1='0' y1='0' x2='0' y2='1'><stop offset='0' stop-color='#ffffff' stop-opacity='.7'/><stop offset='1' stop-color='#ffffff' stop-opacity='.15'/><\/linearGradient><radialGradient id='glow2' cx='.5' cy='1' r='.9'><stop offset='0' stop-color='#ffffff' stop-opacity='.55'/><stop offset='1' stop-color='#ffffff' stop-opacity='0'/><\/radialGradient><linearGradient id='band2' x1='0' x2='1'><stop offset='0' stop-color='#ffffff' stop-opacity='0'/><stop offset='.5' stop-color='#ffffff' stop-opacity='.35'/><stop offset='1' stop-color='#ffffff' stop-opacity='0'/><\/linearGradient><\/defs><rect x='0' y='0' width='800' height='320' fill='var(--peach)'/><rect x='420' y='0' width='380' height='320' fill='url(#glow2)'/><polygon points='268,70 292,70 360,290 60,290' fill='url(#beam2)'/><rect x='300' y='150' width='240' height='90' fill='url(#band2)'/><g fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M40 40 L 40 290 L 340 290 L 340 40 Z' opacity='.85'/><rect x='268' y='60' width='24' height='20'/><path d='M280 60 L 280 80 M268 70 L 292 70' stroke-width='2'/><circle cx='190' cy='166' r='16'/><path d='M190 182 L 190 236'/><path d='M190 196 L 166 214 M190 196 L 214 214'/><path d='M190 236 L 172 288 M190 236 L 208 288'/><path d='M450 288 Q 640 280 780 288' opacity='.5'/><path d='M560 288 q 40 -30 80 0' opacity='.8'/><path d='M600 258 L 600 240 M590 248 L 610 248' stroke-width='2.5'/><g stroke-width='2.5'><circle cx='500' cy='206' r='11'/><path d='M500 217 L 500 258 M500 228 L 488 244 M500 228 L 512 244 M500 258 L 490 288 M500 258 L 510 288'/><circle cx='535' cy='200' r='11'/><path d='M535 211 L 535 254 M535 222 L 524 238 M535 222 L 546 238 M535 254 L 526 288 M535 254 L 544 288'/><circle cx='680' cy='204' r='11'/><path d='M680 215 L 680 256 M680 226 L 668 240 M680 226 L 692 240 M680 256 L 670 288 M680 256 L 690 288'/><circle cx='712' cy='210' r='11'/><path d='M712 221 L 712 258 M712 232 L 702 246 M712 232 L 722 246 M712 258 L 704 288 M712 258 L 720 288'/><\/g><circle cx='760' cy='150' r='14'/><path d='M760 164 L 760 226'/><path d='M760 178 L 744 200 M760 178 L 776 200'/><path d='M760 226 L 748 288 M760 226 L 772 288'/><\/g><g transform='translate(96,9) scale(1.1)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"left":{"lineage":"christian","source":"philippians 4:4 · read the whole letter, it's short, and notice where he's writing from","sourceRef":"chains","text":"rejoice. and again i say it.\n[[paul]] wrote that [[chains|in chains]]. not from a beach. he said it twice in one sentence like he knew you'd argue.\nhappiness isn't a report on your circumstances. it's a decision you make with your circumstances still standing there."},"mode":"disagree","question":"which one do you reach for because it's true, and which one because it's easier?","refs":{"chains":{"lines":"philippians is one of the prison letters. in chapter 1 he says his chains have become known through the whole palace guard, and that being locked up has actually spread the message further, so he's fine with it.\nscholars argue about which prison, rome or ephesus or caesarea. doesn't matter for our purposes. the man who wrote rejoice always was writing it from a cell, to people who were worried about him, and he was the one doing the comforting.\nsame letter, chapter 4, a few lines after the one we used: he says he has learned to be content with plenty and with nothing, well fed and hungry. he wasn't guessing.","name":"in chains","where":"philippians, all four chapters. it takes about fifteen minutes."},"ecclesiastes":{"lines":"the writer calls himself qoheleth, the teacher, or the one who gathers people. son of david, king in jerusalem, which is why tradition says solomon.\nhe tried everything. wisdom, pleasure, wine, building projects, gardens, wealth, women, work. and he calls all of it hevel, a hebrew word that means vapor or breath. something you can see for a second and can't hold. most bibles translate it vanity, which is weaker.\nthe book almost didn't make it in. the rabbis argued over it for a long time. what saved it was the last few verses, fear god and keep the commandments, and plenty of people think somebody nervous added those.\nread it either way. it's the most honest book in there about how little of your life you actually control.","name":"ecclesiastes","where":"ecclesiastes, twelve chapters. start at 1 and don't skip to the ending."},"mourning":{"lines":"chapter 7 opens with a run of better-than sayings. a good name is better than fine oil. the day of death is better than the day of birth. and then this one: better to go to the house of mourning than the house of feasting, because death is where everyone ends up and the living should take it to heart.\nhe keeps going. sorrow is better than laughter. a sad face is good for the heart. the heart of the wise is in the house of mourning, the heart of fools in the house of pleasure.\nthe house of mourning is a funeral, a shiva, the week after. the house of feasting is the party. he isn't against parties. he's against forgetting.","name":"the house of mourning","where":"ecclesiastes 7, the first four verses, then keep reading."},"paul":{"lines":"born saul, a pharisee from tarsus, a tentmaker by trade. he was on the road to damascus to arrest christians when a light knocked him flat and a voice asked why he was doing it. he was blind for three days.\nthen he spent the rest of his life starting small churches around the mediterranean and writing them letters when they got into trouble, which was constantly. thirteen letters in the new testament carry his name.\nhe was beaten, shipwrecked, stoned once and left for dead outside a town, and jailed more than once. tradition says rome finally executed him.\nhe's also the one who stood up in athens and quoted their own greek poets back at them, in acts 17, to tell them the unknown god they'd built an altar to had a name. old texts talking to each other. he did it first.","name":"paul","where":"acts 9 for the road. acts 17 for athens. 2 corinthians 11 for the list of what it cost him, in his own words."}},"right":{"lineage":"christian","source":"ecclesiastes 7:2-4 · read the whole chapter, it doesn't get easier","sourceRef":"mourning","text":"go to the funeral.\n[[ecclesiastes]] says it flat out. the [[mourning|house of mourning]] beats the house of feasting, and the wise keep their heart in the first one.\nnot because sadness is holy. because the party lets you forget you're going to die, and forgetting makes you stupid about what matters."},"theme":"the party or the funeral","id":"2026-09-26","caption":"the light in the cell and the light at the grave are the same light."},{"date":"2026-09-27","doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 800 320' aria-label='a figure sitting by a cart on the left, a road that stops, a figure climbing a fence at night on the right'><defs><linearGradient id='fold1' x1='0' x2='1'><stop offset='0' stop-color='var(--marigold)'/><stop offset='1' stop-color='var(--peach)'/><\/linearGradient><linearGradient id='fade1' x1='0' x2='1'><stop offset='0' stop-color='var(--peach)' stop-opacity='0'/><stop offset='1' stop-color='var(--paper)'/><\/linearGradient><\/defs><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='320' fill='var(--marigold)'/><rect x='400' y='0' width='400' height='320' fill='var(--peach)'/><rect x='340' y='0' width='120' height='320' fill='url(#fold1)'/><rect x='690' y='0' width='110' height='320' fill='url(#fade1)'/><g fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M30 250 Q 120 244 200 250 T 330 248' opacity='.5'/><rect x='58' y='196' width='70' height='40' rx='4'/><circle cx='74' cy='250' r='12'/><circle cx='114' cy='250' r='12'/><path d='M128 206 L 152 190'/><circle cx='210' cy='186' r='16'/><path d='M210 202 L 210 236'/><path d='M210 212 L 190 226 M210 212 L 232 222'/><path d='M210 236 L 178 246 L 206 254 M210 236 L 242 246 L 214 254'/><path d='M150 262 L 300 262' stroke-dasharray='14 12' opacity='.85'/><path d='M300 262 L 330 262' stroke-dasharray='4 8' opacity='.5'/><path d='M334 254 L 334 270' opacity='.5'/><path d='M400 262 L 560 262' stroke-dasharray='14 12' opacity='.85'/><path d='M570 208 L 570 272 M690 208 L 690 272 M560 224 L 700 224 M560 248 L 700 248'/><circle cx='612' cy='158' r='16'/><path d='M612 174 L 612 216'/><path d='M612 186 L 588 198 M612 186 L 636 176'/><path d='M612 216 L 596 254 M612 216 L 652 238 L 660 258'/><path d='M742 60 a 20 20 0 1 0 8 36 a 16 16 0 0 1 -8 -36' opacity='.75'/><path d='M660 62 l 4 0 M700 100 l 4 0 M770 130 l 4 0 M630 40 l 4 0' stroke-width='4' opacity='.7'/><\/g><path d='M186 226 q 10 -50 44 -48' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2' stroke-dasharray='2 6' opacity='.5'/><g transform='translate(676,176) scale(1.15)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><g transform='translate(70,204) scale(1.5)' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><path d='M0 5 Q 4 2 8 3 L 26 3 L 26 8 L 20 8 L 20 11 L 24 11 L 24 15 L 6 15 L 6 11 L 10 11 L 10 8 L 4 8 Z'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"left":{"lineage":"hindu","source":"bhagavad gita 2:47 · read all of chapter 2, not the verse","sourceRef":"gita","text":"you don't get the ending.\n[[arjuna]] sat down in the chariot and wouldn't fight because he could already see how it goes. [[krishna]] didn't argue with the forecast. he told him the results were never his to begin with.\nyou get the work. that's the whole deal. the fruit goes somewhere you don't control."},"mode":"agree","question":"what are you carrying that hasn't happened yet?","refs":{"arjuna":{"lines":"the best archer alive, third of five brothers, and the one the whole gita is talking to.\nthe war is a family fight over a kingdom. his cousins are on the other side. so is bhishma, the great-uncle who helped raise him, and drona, the man who taught him to shoot.\nin chapter one he asks krishna to drive the chariot out between the two armies so he can look. he looks. his hands go weak, the bow slides out, and he sits down on the floor of the chariot and says he won't fight.\nnobody in the story treats that as cowardice. krishna just doesn't accept it as the end of the conversation. the other seventeen chapters are what gets said while arjuna is sitting there.","name":"arjuna","where":"bhagavad gita, chapter 1, which sits inside the mahabharata. any translation. the old edwin arnold one is free everywhere."},"gita":{"lines":"seven hundred verses tucked into the mahabharata, the enormous indian war epic, roughly ten times the length of the iliad and the odyssey put together.\neighteen chapters, all one conversation, held on a battlefield in the minute before the fighting starts. hindus read it as scripture. gandhi read it most mornings. ram dass's whole path runs through it.\nchapter 2 is where krishna starts answering. 2:47 is the verse we used: you have a right to your work, never to its fruits. read the whole chapter and you'll see he's not being gentle about it.","name":"the bhagavad gita","where":"bhagavad gita, chapter 2. any translation you can hold in your hand."},"jesus":{"lines":"a jewish teacher from a small town in galilee, about two thousand years ago. he spent roughly three years walking around telling stories, eating with the wrong people, and healing whoever showed up. the romans executed him.\nchristians say he got up again three days later and that he was god the whole time. you don't have to sign any of that to read him.\nfour short books tell his story: matthew, mark, luke, and john. they don't fully agree with each other on the details, which is part of the honesty of the thing.\nthis book reads him the way a mystic would. less rule-giver, more the guy pointing at the birds and saying look.","name":"jesus","where":"the gospels: matthew, mark, luke, john. start with mark, it's the shortest and the fastest."},"krishna":{"lines":"arjuna's charioteer, friend, and cousin on his mother's side. he agreed to drive but not to fight.\nsomewhere in chapter two it becomes clear he is also god, and in chapter eleven arjuna asks to see that directly and immediately wishes he hadn't. krishna puts the friendly face back on and they keep talking.\nhe never once tells arjuna the fight will go well. he tells him whose the outcome is, and it isn't arjuna's. do the work, hand over the result. that's the line we used, and it's probably the most quoted line in the book.","name":"krishna","where":"bhagavad gita, chapters 2 and 11 for the two faces of him."},"sermon":{"lines":"matthew 5, 6, and 7. jesus goes up a hill, sits down, and talks for three chapters straight.\nit opens with the beatitudes, blessed are the poor in spirit, the ones who mourn, the meek. then anger, lust, oaths, revenge, loving your enemies, giving in secret, the lord's prayer, money, worry, judging other people, and it ends with a house built on rock and one built on sand.\nthe worry part is the end of chapter 6. look at the birds, look at the lilies, they aren't working themselves to death and they're fine. then the line we used. people cut that line into posters. read straight through, it's a lot harder than the posters, because he's just told you to give away your coat and love the people who hit you.","name":"the sermon on the mount","where":"matthew, chapters 5 through 7. any bible, any translation, one sitting."}},"right":{"lineage":"christian","source":"matthew 6:34 · read the whole sermon, chapters 5 through 7","sourceRef":"sermon","text":"you don't get tomorrow either.\n[[jesus]] said [[sermon|don't worry about it]], tomorrow has its own problems, today has enough.\npeople read that as comfort. it isn't. it's a fence. you're not allowed past midnight, and you keep climbing it at 2am anyway."},"theme":"what's yours to carry","id":"2026-09-27","caption":"the road only goes as far as the fence. past that, nothing's been drawn yet."},{"date":"2026-09-28","doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 800 320' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'> <defs> <linearGradient id='sf20260928bg' x1='0' y1='0' x2='1' y2='0'> <stop offset='0' stop-color='var(--peach)'/> <stop offset='0.4' stop-color='var(--peach)'/> <stop offset='0.6' stop-color='var(--blue)'/> <stop offset='1' stop-color='var(--blue)'/> <\/linearGradient> <\/defs> <rect x='0' y='0' width='800' height='320' fill='url(#sf20260928bg)' stroke='none'/> <path d='M 0 285 C 40 275 80 295 120 285 S 200 275 240 285 S 320 295 360 285 S 420 280 460 283 L 770 283'/> <path d='M 90 262 C 88 220 92 180 95 130'/> <path d='M 95 130 C 120 118 150 138 160 205'/> <path d='M 95 130 C 70 116 42 140 46 205'/> <path d='M 95 130 C 108 128 128 150 132 222'/> <path d='M 96 138 C 115 145 130 160 158 168'/> <path d='M 46 190 L 46 216 M 58 190 L 58 216 M 46 190 Q 52 180 58 190 M 50 194 L 50 214 M 54 194 L 54 214'/> <circle cx='250' cy='150' r='14'/> <ellipse cx='257' cy='155' rx='3' ry='4'/> <path d='M 250 164 L 250 220 M 250 220 L 235 260 M 250 220 L 265 260 M 250 182 L 226 150 M 250 182 L 276 158'/> <path d='M 278 138 L 288 132 M 281 150 L 293 150'/> <path d='M 264 146 C 300 128 320 170 355 145 C 385 122 400 142 420 118 C 445 92 480 95 500 125 C 520 155 545 190 555 220 C 560 236 566 244 565 252'/> <path d='M 548 283 L 582 283'/> <path d='M 555 283 C 549 266 568 262 565 252 C 573 262 581 270 578 283'/> <circle cx='540' cy='287' r='2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none' opacity='.5'/> <circle cx='591' cy='286' r='2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none' opacity='.5'/> <circle cx='660' cy='205' r='14'/> <path d='M 655 211 L 665 211'/> <path d='M 660 219 L 660 262 M 660 262 C 640 262 630 280 645 283 M 660 262 C 680 262 690 280 675 283 M 660 236 L 646 262 M 660 236 L 674 262'/> <g transform='translate(128,135) scale(1.1)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g> <\/svg>"},"left":{"lineage":"christian","source":"psalm 137 · read the whole thing, then 88, the one that ends in the dark","sourceRef":"psalm137","text":"say the ugly thing.\nthere's a [[psalms|psalm]] that ends with a man in [[exile]] hoping somebody smashes his enemy's babies on the rocks. [[psalm137|psalm 137]]. they left it in the book.\nyou're allowed to say what you actually feel to god. apparently he's heard worse.\nwhat you swallow doesn't leave. it just moves in."},"mode":"disagree","question":"what are you swallowing right now, and what's it feeding?","refs":{"bodhicaryavatara":{"lines":"shantideva's book. ten chapters, written in verse in the eighth century, on how to become a bodhisattva, which is someone who's waking up not for their own sake but for everybody else's.\nchapter 6 is on patience and it opens with the line about a single moment of anger burning up a thousand eons of good deeds. then it works through every excuse you've got for staying mad, and takes each one apart.\nthe famous image from the book is in chapter 5: you'll never find enough leather to cover the whole earth, but with leather on the soles of your feet it's as if you had. you don't fix the world. you fix where you stand on it.\nfor today, the book is the strongest case in any tradition for not saying the thing. not swallowing it either. just not feeding it.","name":"the way of the bodhisattva","where":"chapter 6, patience. read the first ten verses, then the stretch near the end of the chapter where the enemy becomes the teacher."},"exile":{"lines":"in 586 bc the babylonian army under nebuchadnezzar took jerusalem, burned the temple, and marched the leadership of judah hundreds of miles east to babylon. that's the exile. it lasted about fifty years.\nit was the worst thing that had ever happened to those people. the temple was where god lived, as far as they knew. now it was rubble and they were singing in a foreign country.\na lot of the hebrew bible got its final shape in that stretch, because people were trying to figure out how to be who they were with nothing left. then the persian king cyrus took babylon and let them go home.\npsalm 137 was written inside that wound. the anger in it isn't a thought experiment. it's people who watched their city die.","name":"the exile","where":"2 kings 24 and 25 tell the fall. lamentations is the grief. psalm 137 is the anger. ezra 1 is the trip home."},"patience":{"lines":"one of the six perfections in mahayana buddhism, the qualities a bodhisattva practices: generosity, discipline, patience, effort, meditation, wisdom. the sanskrit word is kshanti.\nit doesn't mean gritting your teeth and waiting. it means not getting knocked off center by what hurts you, and not making it worse. shantideva says there's no austerity like it, no practice that does more.\nthe trick in his version is that you can't practice it in a nice room. it only exists in the presence of somebody who's pissing you off. that's why he calls the enemy a treasure found lying in your house.\nfor today: the right page isn't telling you to be nice. it's telling you the person you want to say the ugly thing to is your practice, and the fire goes out on its own if you stop feeding it.","name":"patience (kshanti)","where":"the way of the bodhisattva, chapter 6, all of it."},"psalm137":{"lines":"a song written by people who'd just lost everything. jerusalem burned, the temple gone, and them marched off to babylon. it opens with the line most people know: by the rivers of babylon we sat down and wept. their captors ask them to sing one of the old songs for fun, and they hang their harps in the trees and refuse.\nthen the last two verses turn. the singer asks god to remember what was done to his city, and says whoever grabs babylon's babies and smashes them on the rocks will be happy. that's the end of the psalm. no apology, no take-back.\nnobody edited it out. it's been in the prayer book of jews and christians for twenty five hundred years, and monks still chant it. scholars call these the imprecatory psalms, the cursing ones, and there are more of them than you'd think.\nit matters today because the book makes room for rage. not tidy rage. the real kind. and it puts that rage in front of god instead of behind your teeth.","name":"psalm 137","where":"the book of psalms, psalm 137. then read psalm 88, the only one that ends without a way out."},"psalms":{"lines":"a hundred and fifty songs and poems sitting in the middle of the bible. they were the hymnbook of ancient israel, and later the prayer book of jesus, of paul, of the monks, of pretty much everyone since.\na lot of them are credited to king david, though not all, and plenty are older or later. they were meant to be sung, not read quietly.\nabout a third of them are laments. complaints, really. why have you left me, how long, where are you. the praise ones get quoted on coffee mugs. the lament ones are where the honesty lives.\nfor today: the psalms are proof that saying the worst thing out loud to god was normal, expected, and written down for everyone to sing.","name":"the psalms","where":"the book of psalms, dead center of the bible. try 23, 51, 88, and 137 back to back to get the range."},"shantideva":{"lines":"a buddhist monk in india in the eighth century, living at nalanda, the great monastery and university of the day. the story goes the other monks thought he was useless. he seemed to do nothing but eat, sleep, and go to the toilet, and they gave him a nickname that meant exactly that.\nso they set him up. they asked him to recite scripture in front of everyone, figuring he'd have nothing. he asked if they wanted something old or something new. they said new. he sat down and recited the whole of the way of the bodhisattva from memory, and the legend says partway through the wisdom chapter he rose into the air and was gone.\nwhether that happened or not, the book is real and it's the one tibetan teachers reach for first when they want to talk about how to actually live this stuff. the dalai lama teaches from it constantly.\nhis chapter on patience is the hardest thing in it. he doesn't argue anger is bad. he argues it's expensive, and that the people who hurt you are the only ones who ever give you a place to practice.","name":"shantideva","where":"the way of the bodhisattva, also called the bodhicaryavatara. the padmakara translation reads clean. the dalai lama's book healing anger is a commentary on chapter 6."}},"right":{"lineage":"buddhist","source":"shantideva, the way of the bodhisattva, chapter 6 · read the whole chapter, it's on patience","sourceRef":"bodhicaryavatara","text":"don't feed it.\n[[shantideva]] was a monk in india in the eighth century, and he said one flash of anger burns up a thousand lifetimes of good. everything you built. one match.\nhe also said the guy who wronged you is the only one who ever hands you a chance at [[patience]]. so quit trying to get rid of him.\nanger needs a mouth to stay alive. close it."},"theme":"say it or starve it","id":"2026-09-28","caption":"what one of them shouts is the smoke the other lets burn off."},{"date":"2026-09-29","doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 800 320' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><defs><linearGradient id='sf20260929bg' x1='0' y1='0' x2='1' y2='0'><stop offset='0' stop-color='var(--clay)'/><stop offset='0.4' stop-color='var(--clay)'/><stop offset='0.6' stop-color='var(--peach)'/><stop offset='1' stop-color='var(--peach)'/><\/linearGradient><\/defs><rect x='0' y='0' width='800' height='320' fill='url(#sf20260929bg)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 0 272 C 60 262 110 280 170 272 S 290 262 340 270 C 370 274 390 272 410 272 L 790 270'/><path d='M 60 248 L 68 272 L 118 272 L 126 248 Z'/><path d='M 66 248 C 80 218 106 218 120 248'/><circle cx='86' cy='266' r='4' fill='var(--peach)'/><circle cx='101' cy='267' r='4' fill='var(--peach)'/><circle cx='176' cy='158' r='14'/><path d='M 181 164 L 190 168'/><path d='M 178 172 C 181 190 183 206 184 222'/><path d='M 184 222 L 170 268 L 140 268'/><path d='M 184 222 L 214 248 L 214 268'/><path d='M 181 186 L 208 236'/><path d='M 181 186 C 200 194 218 200 232 212'/><path d='M 232 212 L 238 206 M 232 212 L 240 212 M 232 212 L 237 218'/><path d='M 262 270 C 262 258 262 250 264 240'/><path d='M 263 250 C 253 250 247 242 249 236 C 255 238 261 244 263 250'/><path d='M 263 248 C 273 248 279 240 277 234 C 271 236 265 242 263 248'/><circle cx='265' cy='237' r='5' fill='var(--peach)'/><path d='M 305 270 C 305 258 305 250 307 240'/><path d='M 306 250 C 296 250 290 242 292 236 C 298 238 304 244 306 250'/><path d='M 306 248 C 316 248 322 240 320 234 C 314 236 308 242 306 248'/><circle cx='308' cy='237' r='5' fill='var(--peach)'/><path d='M 348 271 C 348 259 348 251 350 241'/><path d='M 349 251 C 339 251 333 243 335 237 C 341 239 347 245 349 251'/><path d='M 349 249 C 359 249 365 241 363 235 C 357 237 351 243 349 249'/><circle cx='351' cy='238' r='5' fill='var(--peach)'/><path d='M 400 272 C 400 264 400 260 401 250'/><path d='M 401 258 C 394 258 389 252 391 247 C 396 249 400 253 401 258'/><path d='M 401 257 C 408 257 413 251 411 246 C 406 248 402 252 401 257'/><circle cx='402' cy='249' r='4' fill='var(--paper)'/><g fill='var(--paper)' stroke='none'><circle cx='372' cy='266' r='2'/><circle cx='386' cy='268' r='2'/><circle cx='414' cy='266' r='2.5'/><circle cx='426' cy='268' r='2.5'/><circle cx='440' cy='265' r='2.5'/><circle cx='452' cy='268' r='2.5'/><circle cx='466' cy='266' r='2.5'/><circle cx='534' cy='266' r='2.5'/><circle cx='548' cy='268' r='2.5'/><circle cx='562' cy='265' r='2.5'/><circle cx='700' cy='266' r='2.5'/><circle cx='716' cy='268' r='2.5'/><circle cx='732' cy='265' r='2.5'/><circle cx='748' cy='267' r='2.5'/><circle cx='764' cy='265' r='2.5'/><circle cx='480' cy='262' r='2.5'/><circle cx='690' cy='262' r='2.5'/><\/g><circle cx='730' cy='84' r='22'/><path d='M 730 50 L 730 42 M 764 84 L 772 84 M 754 60 L 760 54 M 706 60 L 700 54'/><path d='M 560 270 L 620 182 L 680 270'/><path d='M 620 182 L 620 270'/><path d='M 600 270 L 620 236 L 640 270'/><circle cx='498' cy='166' r='14'/><path d='M 505 170 L 511 172'/><path d='M 498 180 L 498 234 M 498 234 L 484 270 M 498 234 L 512 270'/><path d='M 498 196 L 530 206 M 498 196 L 528 214'/><path d='M 532 202 L 528 224 L 552 224 L 548 202 Z'/><path d='M 530 202 L 550 202'/><path d='M 536 196 C 530 186 542 178 536 168 C 530 158 540 150 534 140'/><path d='M 544 196 C 552 184 540 176 548 164 C 556 152 544 144 550 132'/><path d='M 540 192 C 534 178 552 170 542 156 C 532 142 556 132 540 120 C 524 108 500 108 480 112 C 458 116 440 124 424 128'/><g transform='translate(608,155) scale(1.0)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"left":{"lineage":"native","source":"robin wall kimmerer, braiding sweetgrass · read the honorable harvest, then the gift of strawberries","sourceRef":"sweetgrass","text":"leave the first one.\n[[kimmerer|robin wall kimmerer]] is a botanist and a member of the [[potawatomi|citizen potawatomi nation]], and the rules she was raised on for taking anything from the ground start like this: ask first. never take the first one you see. it might be the only one.\nthen take only what you need, only what's given, and say thank you like you mean it. the [[harvest|honorable harvest]], she calls it. none of it is written down. you learn it by watching somebody do it.\nyou were taught to grab what you want before somebody else does. the plants have been here longer than you. you're a guest at their table."},"mode":"agree","question":"what are you keeping that was only ever given for today?","refs":{"exodus":{"lines":"the second book of the bible, and the story of the israelites getting out of egypt. the name is greek for the way out.\nthe shape: chapters 1 through 15 are the escape, plagues, the sea. chapter 16 is the manna, three days after the singing on the far shore, when the food ran out and everyone wished they were back in egypt. 17 is water from a rock. 19 through 24 is mount sinai and the ten commandments. the rest is instructions for building a tent for god to live in.\nit reads fast until the tent. the manna chapter is one of the ones that stays with people: the bread is free, the only rule is don't hoard it, and they hoard it.\nfor today, read the whole of 16 and notice the pattern. complaint, gift, rule, broken rule, and god still shows up the next morning.","name":"exodus","where":"the book of exodus, chapter 16. then 15:22 to 27 for the complaint right before it."},"harvest":{"lines":"kimmerer's name for the rules her elders taught about taking anything from the living world: plants, animals, water, wood. she says they aren't written down anywhere, they're passed by story and by example, and she writes them out in the chapter with that title.\nthe gist: know the ones who take care of you. introduce yourself. ask permission before you take, and abide by the answer. never take the first one. never take the last. take only what you need, and only what's given. leave some for others. use it with respect. never waste it. share. give thanks. give something back. sustain the ones who sustain you.\nshe puts it next to the way a market works, where the point of taking is owning, and there's no such thing as enough. she doesn't pretend she's above that either. she writes about walking into a store and trying to hold the same rules there.\nfor today, the first rule is the one that stings. never take the first one you see. you'll want to. that's the point.","name":"the honorable harvest","where":"braiding sweetgrass, the chapter called the honorable harvest."},"israelites":{"lines":"the people of the hebrew bible, descended in the story from a man named jacob, whose other name was israel. his twelve sons became twelve tribes. they went down to egypt during a famine, stayed, and over generations became slaves there.\nthe exodus is the story of getting out. after that they're the people at the foot of mount sinai, the people in the desert, and eventually the people of the land of israel. the jewish people today trace themselves back through them, and christians read their story as the front half of their own.\nin the desert chapters they are not heroic. they complain about water, about food, about moses, about having left egypt at all. the manna shows up in direct answer to one of those complaints. then they break the one rule about it. that's the whole book in miniature.\nfor today, they're you, the morning after you kept some.","name":"the israelites","where":"genesis 32 for jacob getting the name, exodus 1 for how they ended up enslaved, exodus 16 for the manna and the grumbling."},"jesus":{"lines":"a jewish teacher in roman occupied galilee about two thousand years ago, executed by the romans, and the person christianity is named after. the four gospels are the record of what he said and did, written down in the decades after.\nhe taught his followers one prayer, in matthew 6. one line of it asks for bread for today, not for the week. read next to the manna it's the same rule: enough for now, don't stockpile. in the same sermon he says don't worry about tomorrow, it has enough trouble of its own.\nhe also had a habit of feeding people. in john 6 he feeds a crowd with a few loaves and afterward tells his people to gather up the leftovers so nothing is wasted. take only what's given, and waste none of it. kimmerer's rules, from the other side of the world.\nfor today, he's the one who took the manna story and turned it into a prayer you can say in five seconds.","name":"jesus","where":"matthew 6 for the prayer and the bit about tomorrow. john 6 for the bread and the manna argument."},"kimmerer":{"lines":"a botanist who teaches at the state university of new york's college of environmental science and forestry in syracuse, and an enrolled member of the citizen potawatomi nation. she's been both of those things her whole career, and for a long time the two didn't talk to each other.\nshe tells the story of showing up to college wanting to study why asters and goldenrod look so good together, and being told by her advisor that wasn't science. it took her years to find her way back to the question. her book braiding sweetgrass, published in 2013, is the answer: plants as teachers, and science and indigenous knowledge as two ways of knowing that need each other.\nthe book sold slowly and then, seven years later, hit the bestseller lists on word of mouth alone. she got a macarthur fellowship in 2022. she also founded the center for native peoples and the environment at her school.\nfor today, she's the one who wrote down the rules for taking, and admitted she's had to learn them slowly, one strawberry at a time.","name":"robin wall kimmerer","where":"braiding sweetgrass, milkweed editions, 2013. start with the gift of strawberries, near the front, then the honorable harvest, further in."},"manna":{"lines":"the bread that showed up on the ground in the desert. exodus 16 says when the morning dew lifted there was a fine flaky stuff on the ground, white, and it tasted like wafers made with honey. the people looked at it and said, what is it. in hebrew that's roughly man hu, and the name stuck.\nthe rules were strict. one measure per person per day. gather in the morning, because it melted when the sun got hot. keep none overnight, except on the sixth day, when you gathered double and it kept through the sabbath. anybody who tried to hoard on a normal day found worms in it by morning.\nthe book says it fed them for forty years and stopped the day they ate the first crop from the promised land. a jar of it was supposed to be kept in the ark of the covenant so later generations would know.\njesus gets asked about it in john 6. people want a sign like manna, and he tells them the manna was never the point. your ancestors ate it and died anyway. that's the mystic's reading: it was never about the bread.","name":"manna","where":"exodus 16, the whole chapter. then numbers 11, where they get sick of it, and john 6, where jesus argues about it."},"moses":{"lines":"the man who walked the israelites out of slavery in egypt, and then spent forty years in the desert with them, mostly getting complained at. he's the central human figure of the hebrew bible, and the first five books are traditionally credited to him.\nhis story: born to slaves, hidden in a basket on the nile, raised in pharaoh's own house. killed an egyptian who was beating a hebrew and ran for the desert. spent forty years herding sheep there. then a bush caught fire and didn't burn up, and a voice sent him back to egypt to get everyone out.\nhe wasn't a natural. he told god he couldn't talk right and to send somebody else. later he lost his temper and hit a rock he was told to speak to, and for that he was told he'd never enter the land. he died on a mountain looking at it from across the river.\nfor today, he's the guy delivering the rule about the manna, and the first to watch people ignore it. he'd already learned in the desert, alone, how little you need in a day.","name":"moses","where":"exodus 2 through 4 for the beginning, exodus 16 for the manna, numbers 20 for the rock, deuteronomy 34 for the end."},"potawatomi":{"lines":"a native nation from the great lakes, originally around what's now michigan, wisconsin, indiana and illinois. they're one of the three fires, alongside the ojibwe and the odawa, related peoples who speak related languages. the potawatomi were the keepers of the fire in that alliance.\nin the 1830s the united states pushed them off their land. one group was marched at gunpoint from indiana to kansas in 1838, and enough people died on the way that the potawatomi still call it the trail of death. later they were moved again, to oklahoma. the citizen potawatomi nation is headquartered in shawnee, oklahoma today.\nkimmerer writes that her grandfather was taken to the carlisle indian school in pennsylvania as a boy, where the point was to strip the language and the ways out of native kids. so the potawatomi she carries came to her partly broken, and she's spent her adult life learning it back, including the language, a few words at a time.\nit matters today because the rules on the left page aren't an idea. they're a people's way of staying alive on land, kept through two removals and a boarding school.","name":"the potawatomi","where":"kimmerer tells her family's part in braiding sweetgrass, in the chapter called learning the grammar of animacy, and in the one on her grandfather."},"sweetgrass":{"lines":"robin wall kimmerer's 2013 book, published by milkweed editions, a small nonprofit press in minneapolis. the full title is braiding sweetgrass: indigenous wisdom, scientific knowledge, and the teachings of plants.\nsweetgrass is a fragrant grass that's sacred to a lot of native nations. it gets braided in three strands, and the book is built the same way: her people's teachings, her training as a scientist, and what the plants themselves have to say. the sections are named for working the grass: planting, tending, picking, braiding, burning.\nit's essays, not a manual. one is about wild strawberries and what a gift is. one is about her potawatomi language teacher and why the language has a word for a bay that's a verb, not a noun. one is the honorable harvest. one is the thanksgiving address the haudenosaunee open their gatherings with, which goes on for a while because it thanks everything.\nthe thread through all of it is reciprocity. the world gives, and the only sane response is to give back. that's the whole left page.","name":"braiding sweetgrass","where":"the book itself. the gift of strawberries is near the front. the honorable harvest is in the section called braiding sweetgrass. read those two and you'll know if you want the rest."},"wilderness":{"lines":"the desert between egypt and the land the israelites had been promised, the sinai peninsula, roughly. the trip should have taken about eleven days. deuteronomy says so, flat out. it took forty years.\nwhy forty years: when they got to the edge of the land they sent scouts in, the scouts came back scared, and the people refused to go. so the whole generation that walked out of egypt died in the desert and their kids went in instead. the wandering wasn't the plan. it was the consequence.\ndeuteronomy 8 looks back on it and says the hunger and the manna were there to teach one thing: a person doesn't live on bread alone. centuries later jesus spends forty days hungry in a desert and quotes that exact line back at the devil. old texts talking to each other.\nfor today, the wilderness is where the manna rule gets learned, because it's the one place nobody could pretend they were feeding themselves.","name":"the wilderness","where":"exodus 15 through 18 for the first stretch, numbers 13 and 14 for the refusal, deuteronomy 8 for the point of it, matthew 4 for jesus quoting it."}},"right":{"lineage":"christian","source":"exodus 16 · read the whole chapter, and count how many times they complain","sourceRef":"exodus","text":"it rots by morning.\nthe [[israelites]] were starving in the [[wilderness]] and bread showed up on the ground at dawn. [[manna]]. [[moses]] told them to gather what they'd eat that day and keep none of it.\nsome of them kept some. by morning it was full of worms and it stank.\nover a thousand years later [[jesus]] taught people to pray for bread one day at a time. same lesson. whatever you're holding back for later is doing what the manna did. you just can't smell it yet."},"theme":"only what's given, only today","id":"2026-09-29","caption":"what one of them left alone is growing. what the other one kept is rotting."},{"date":"2026-09-30","doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 800 320' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><defs><linearGradient id='sf20260930bg' x1='0' y1='0' x2='1' y2='0'><stop offset='0' stop-color='var(--blue)'/><stop offset='0.4' stop-color='var(--blue)'/><stop offset='0.6' stop-color='var(--peach)'/><stop offset='1' stop-color='var(--peach)'/><\/linearGradient><\/defs><rect x='0' y='0' width='800' height='320' fill='url(#sf20260930bg)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='330' cy='58' r='17' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 612 192 L 616 306 L 712 306 L 716 192 Z' fill='var(--paper)' stroke='none' opacity='.45'/><path d='M 0 192 C 70 188 150 196 230 192 S 370 190 430 192 L 612 192 L 616 306 L 712 306 L 716 192 L 800 192'/><path d='M 34 192 C 33 150 35 100 34 48'/><path d='M 28 152 L 40 152 M 28 112 L 40 112 M 28 78 L 40 78'/><path d='M 98 192 C 99 150 97 110 98 70'/><path d='M 92 160 L 104 160 M 92 124 L 104 124 M 92 94 L 104 94'/><path d='M 64 192 L 64 152'/><path d='M 57 152 L 71 152 M 58 174 L 70 174'/><path d='M 34 70 C 44 61 57 61 64 68 C 55 75 43 75 34 70 Z' fill='var(--sage)'/><path d='M 34 122 C 26 113 14 113 7 120 C 15 127 26 127 34 122 Z' fill='var(--sage)'/><path d='M 98 104 C 108 95 121 95 128 102 C 119 109 107 109 98 104 Z' fill='var(--sage)'/><circle cx='230' cy='104' r='14'/><path d='M 217 106 L 210 108'/><path d='M 230 118 L 228 160'/><path d='M 228 160 L 211 192 M 228 160 L 246 192'/><path d='M 229 132 L 209 146 M 229 132 L 245 151'/><path d='M 210 124 L 204 192'/><path d='M 258 201 C 300 205 350 203 398 201' stroke-dasharray='1 13'/><path d='M 400 201 C 450 199 520 192 560 177 C 580 169 592 164 604 162 C 616 168 624 184 628 200 C 632 216 638 228 645 236'/><circle cx='580' cy='118' r='14'/><path d='M 580 132 L 582 168'/><path d='M 582 168 L 571 192 M 582 168 L 595 192'/><path d='M 581 144 L 604 160 M 581 150 L 600 167'/><circle cx='676' cy='256' r='14'/><path d='M 676 270 L 676 292'/><path d='M 676 292 L 666 306 M 676 292 L 687 306'/><path d='M 676 275 L 656 262 L 648 240 M 676 275 L 660 271 L 652 247'/><g transform='translate(52,125) scale(1.0)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"left":{"lineage":"buddhist","source":"khaggavisana sutta, sutta nipata 1.3 · read all forty one verses, it takes ten minutes","sourceRef":"rhino","text":"walk alone.\nthere's an old poem in the [[palicanon|pali canon]], forty one verses, and nearly every one ends on the same line: wander alone, like a [[rhino|rhinoceros]].\nit's kept with the [[buddha]]'s teachings, and its reasons aren't gentle. you get close to people, you start needing them, and the needing is where it hurts. it says family tangles you up like a bamboo thicket. be the one shoot that grows clear of it.\nnobody can walk this for you. quit waiting for company."},"mode":"disagree","question":"which one are you hiding in right now, the crowd or the cave?","refs":{"bonhoeffer":{"lines":"a german pastor and theologian, born in 1906, hanged by the nazis in april 1945 at thirty nine. he was one of eight children, had his doctorate at twenty one, and could have had a quiet career writing books.\ninstead he was against hitler in public from the first days. two days after hitler took office he was on the radio warning about him, and the broadcast got cut off. when much of the german church went along with the nazis, he joined the pastors who refused, and ended up training their ministers in secret. in the summer of 1939 he left for new york, where he'd have been safe. he turned around within weeks and sailed home, because he didn't think he'd have the right to help rebuild germany if he sat the war out.\nback home he joined the resistance, with a job in military intelligence as his cover. he was arrested in april 1943 and spent two years locked up. they hanged him at the flossenbürg camp on april 9, 1945, two weeks before american soldiers got there.\nhere's why he's on this page. the man who wrote that you can't do it alone spent his last two years in prison, cut off from every one of those men. what came out of it was letters, and most of the ones in the book made from them were written to one friend, eberhard bethge, who'd been his student at the seminary. the community was gone. he kept one brother, by mail.","name":"dietrich bonhoeffer","where":"life together for the community. letters and papers from prison for the cell. eberhard bethge's dietrich bonhoeffer: a biography is the full story, written by the friend who got the letters."},"buddha":{"lines":"siddhattha gotama, born about twenty five hundred years ago near what's now the border of nepal and india. buddha isn't a name. it's a title, and it means the one who woke up.\nthe story as it's handed down: he had a comfortable home, a wife, a son. at twenty nine he left all of it, cut off his hair, and went looking for the end of suffering. he studied under two teachers and outgrew both. he spent years starving himself alongside five companions, and when he quit that and ate a real meal, the five walked out on him in disgust. he was by himself when he sat down under the tree and woke up. he was thirty five.\nthen he did the opposite for forty five years. he went and found those same five and taught them first. he built a community of monks and nuns, and he walked the roads of north india with a crowd around him until he died at eighty.\nso he lived both pages of this spread. he got free alone, and then he never lived alone again. the rhinoceros verses sound like the first half of that life.","name":"the buddha","where":"the ariyapariyesana sutta, majjhima nikaya 26, is his own account of leaving home, the two teachers, the five companions, and waking up. the mahaparinibbana sutta, digha nikaya 16, is the last months."},"finkenwalde":{"lines":"a village near the city of stettin, close to the baltic coast. germany then, poland now. a seminary is a school that trains pastors, and in 1935 the confessing church, the german protestants who wouldn't let the nazi state run their church, needed one of its own. they gave the job to bonhoeffer. he was twenty nine.\nhe ran it like nothing his students had seen. twenty three young men that first term, in a neglected old estate they had to clean out before they could live in it. the day started in silence. then psalms read back and forth, and scripture out loud, morning and night. after breakfast, half an hour alone and quiet over one short passage.\npeople sneered that he'd started a monastery. some of his own students joked about it and found it heavy going. he thought protestants had thrown out something they needed.\nthe state declared the training illegal, and in the fall of 1937 the gestapo closed the place and sealed it. by the end of that year twenty seven of his former students had been arrested. he kept the training going in secret for a couple more years, in a small town and a village further east.\nso the book on this page is about something that lasted a little over two years and was already gone when he wrote it down.","name":"finkenwalde","where":"bethge's dietrich bonhoeffer: a biography, the chapters on the preachers' seminary. bonhoeffer's own version of what they did there is life together."},"gestapo":{"lines":"the secret police of nazi germany. the name is short for geheime staatspolizei, secret state police. hitler took power in 1933 and ran germany until 1945, and the gestapo was how the state dealt with anyone it decided was an enemy. no court had to sign off. they could lock you up on their own say so.\nit was a much smaller outfit than its reputation. most of its cases started with a tip from an ordinary german, a neighbor reporting a neighbor, and the fear did the rest.\na state that wanted to be the only thing anybody answered to had no use for pastors who wouldn't go along. they got watched, silenced and jailed. bonhoeffer lost his right to teach at the university in 1936, was banned from speaking in public in 1940, and from publishing in 1941.\nfor today: the thing that ended his community wasn't an argument or a falling out. it was police at the door. he knew, when he wrote the book, that having your people around you is not a given.","name":"the gestapo","where":"bethge's biography tells the closing of the seminary. bonhoeffer's own line on community being grace, nothing you can claim, is in the first chapter of life together."},"lifetogether":{"lines":"a short book bonhoeffer wrote in 1938, the year after the gestapo closed finkenwalde. he wrote it in the empty home of his twin sister sabine. her husband's father was jewish, which made the whole family jews in nazi eyes, and that fall they fled germany. bonhoeffer drove them to the swiss border himself.\nit's what he learned living with his students, put on paper. five chapters: community, the day with others, the day alone, ministry, and confession and communion. when he says brother he means any other christian, but you can read it as whoever is actually walking this with you.\nthe first chapter is where today's page comes from. he says it isn't a given that you get to live among your own people. jesus lived in the middle of his enemies. so if you've got people, that's grace, nothing you can claim. then the hard part: you need another person to say the true thing to you, because in your own heart it's shaky, and in his mouth it's sure.\nhe's rough on the dream version too. whoever loves his dream of a community more than the actual community, he says, ends up destroying it, however honest and earnest he is about it.\nthen the chapter on the day alone turns around and argues the left page. whoever can't be alone should beware of community. whoever isn't in community should beware of being alone. he sets the two warnings back to back and won't pick.","name":"life together","where":"life together, the first chapter, community, for the brother's word being stronger than your own. the third, the day alone, for the two warnings. it's short enough to read in an evening."},"palicanon":{"lines":"the oldest complete set of buddhist scriptures still in use. pali is an old indian language, a cousin of sanskrit, and this is the collection kept by the theravada school, the buddhism of sri lanka, thailand and burma.\nnobody wrote it down for centuries. monks memorized it and chanted it together, which is why it repeats itself so much. repeating is how you don't lose a line. it was finally put in writing in sri lanka around the first century bc, on palm leaves.\nit's called the three baskets. one holds the rules for monks and nuns. one holds the teachings, thousands of talks and poems. one holds the later analysis, which reads like a parts manual for the mind.\nthe rhinoceros poem sits in the teachings basket, in a small book called the sutta nipata, and scholars count parts of that book among the earliest layers of the whole thing. read it and you're about as close to the first generation as the record gets.","name":"the pali canon","where":"the sutta nipata is one of the short books in the khuddaka nikaya, the last of the five collections of teachings. thanissaro bhikkhu and bhikkhu bodhi have both put the whole sutta nipata into english."},"rhino":{"lines":"a sutta is a teaching of the buddha, memorized and handed down. most are talks. this one is a poem, forty one verses, called the khaggavisana sutta in pali, the language it was kept in. it's counted among the oldest material in the whole collection. a copy on birch bark from about two thousand years ago, from the old region of gandhara and now in the british library, is among the oldest buddhist manuscripts anyone has.\nthe refrain is the whole poem. wander alone like a rhinoceros. translators argue about it, because the pali word can mean the animal or its horn, and the indian rhino has just the one. either way the picture is the same. one, not two.\nthe verses go after everything you lean on. friends, children, a spouse, good company. then come the pictures of what free looks like. a deer in the woods that eats where it wants. a lion that doesn't jump at noises. wind a net can't hold. a lotus the water doesn't stick to.\nhere's what the hit line leaves out. one spot breaks the pattern. if you find a companion who's wise and steady, walk with him and be glad. if you don't, go alone, like a king walking away from his kingdom. so the poem was never against people. it was against company that makes you smaller.\ntwo more things. the tradition's own old commentary says these verses weren't first spoken by the buddha. it credits them to solitary buddhas, people who woke up on their own and never taught. and the buddha himself, in another sutta, corrects his attendant ananda for saying good friends are half the path. not half, he says. the whole of it. the collection kept both and never smoothed it over.","name":"the rhinoceros sutta","where":"sutta nipata, chapter 1, sutta 3, verses 35 to 75. the exchange with ananda about friendship is the upaddha sutta, samyutta nikaya 45.2."}},"right":{"lineage":"christian","source":"dietrich bonhoeffer, life together · read the first chapter, then the day alone, where he argues the other side","sourceRef":"lifetogether","text":"you can't do this alone.\n[[bonhoeffer]] ran an illegal [[finkenwalde|seminary]] in germany until the [[gestapo]] shut it. then he sat down and wrote [[lifetogether|a short book]] about what living with those men had taught him.\nthe truth in your own head is weak. the same truth out of your brother's mouth is strong. you can't talk yourself out of the hole, because the one doing the talking is the one in it.\nhe called having people around you grace, nothing you can claim. his got taken."},"theme":"walk alone, walk together","id":"2026-09-30","caption":"the trail one of them walked alone is the rope the other gets pulled out with."}];
